 {"id":"CF-143","title":"P3 — the canonical board's append-only ledger was rewritten in place: df0c8fc re-serialised 35 historical event lines and no gate fails on it","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-04 in the canonical checkout at 2a3d357, by the duckbrain memory lane of tick code-factory-duckbrain-2026-11-04-01-21-33.\n\nAGENTS.md and docs/BOARD.md both describe the board as append-only, one JSON object per line, and the fleet's skill doctrine forbids rewriting a tracked JSONL board through a JSON slurp/dump. Commit df0c8fc (\"docs(pm): re-anchor the PRD milestone and the acceptance plan to the live tip; file CF-141\") rewrote 35 pre-existing lines of .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl while resolving the rebase this checkout was in at 20:51 -05: `git show --numstat df0c8fc -- .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl` reports 36 insertions / 35 deletions against a one-line row addition in tasks.jsonl.\n\nThe content survived. Canonicalising both sides (`git show <rev>:<path> | jq -c . | sort`) and diffing gives a strict subset: additions only (event ids 163, 164, 165), zero deleted events. What changed is the raw bytes of historical lines — the legacy rows written as `{\"id\": 1, \"timestamp\": ...}` (space after the colon) now read compact. So this is byte churn on an append-only ledger, not loss of evidence, and it is exactly the shape the fleet doctrine warns about: a resolver that reparses and rewrites the whole file is indistinguishable, from the commit, from a legitimate line-wise merge, and it leaves no trace that anything historical moved.\n\nWhy it matters even without data loss: every later audit that compares the ledger's bytes, or that reconstructs \"what did this commit actually change\" from numstat, sees 35 phantom deletions in a docs commit; a future real deletion would look identical; and the append-only promise that makes the ledger auditable is asserted nowhere a machine can check. The git-tracked JSONL doctrine exists precisely to keep untouched lines byte-identical, and this repo has no guard that enforces it on events.jsonl.\n\nThe probe is a detector by design (it prints offending commits and exits 0 on the current tree):\ngit log --format='commit %h' --numstat -- .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl | awk '/^commit /{c=$2; next} NF==3 && $2+0>0 {print c\": \"$0}'\nIt prints exactly one line today: `df0c8fc: 36\t35\t.coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl`. Acceptance criterion 3 asks for a gate that reds on the NEXT occurrence (a fixture that deletes an event line must red; the current tree must stay green).\n\nNot claimed: which step produced the rewrite. A line-wise rebase resolution, a `git checkout --ours` followed by a re-dump, and a hand re-serialisation all leave the same numstat; the commit cannot distinguish them. The row is about the unguarded class, not about attributing intent.\n\nRelated, not duplicates: CF-116 (a census coverage assertion mints a duplicate id on a mid-run insert), CF-130 (complete rows with no completion transition), CF-081 (hand-typed measured-state block). None of them notices a rewrite of pre-existing ledger lines.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree, from the repository root: the row's probe prints exactly one line, `df0c8fc: 36\t35\t.coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl`, and exits 0. (The probe is a detector, not a failing check: its output names every commit that deleted lines from the append-only ledger.)\n1. `git show --numstat df0c8fc -- .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl` reports 36 insertions and 35 deletions, and `git diff f49d85b..df0c8fc -- .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl | grep -c '^-{'` is 35 — the deletions exist at the raw byte level.\n2. The deletions are formatting-only, proven rather than assumed: `git show f49d85b:.coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl | jq -c . | sort` is a strict subset of the same pipeline at `df0c8fc` (0 deleted lines in the canonicalised comparison, 3 added), so no event was lost and